# Three.js in Node.js (Headless) Setup

## Overview
Three.js is primarily a browser-based 3D library, but it can be used in Node.js for headless operations like geometry processing, scene graph manipulation, and file export — without requiring a GPU or display.

## Installation
```bash
npm install three
```

## Key Considerations
- Three.js uses ES modules. Use `import` syntax or dynamic `import()`.
- Node.js does not have a DOM or WebGL context, so rendering is not possible, but scene graph construction and geometry manipulation work fine.
- When running scripts, use `"type": "module"` in `package.json` or use `.mjs` extension.

## Usage Pattern for Headless Scene Construction
```javascript
import * as THREE from 'three';

// Create geometries, meshes, groups — all work without a renderer
const group = new THREE.Group();
const geometry = new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1);
const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry);
group.add(mesh);
```

## Handling InstancedMesh
`InstancedMesh` stores per-instance transforms in an `instanceMatrix` (a `Float32Array`). Each instance is a 4x4 matrix. To extract individual meshes from an `InstancedMesh`:

```javascript
const instanced = new THREE.InstancedMesh(geometry, material, count);
const matrix = new THREE.Matrix4();
for (let i = 0; i < instanced.count; i++) {
  instanced.getMatrixAt(i, matrix);
  // Decompose or apply matrix to clone geometry
  const pos = new THREE.Vector3();
  const quat = new THREE.Quaternion();
  const scale = new THREE.Vector3();
  matrix.decompose(pos, quat, scale);
}
```

## Coordinate System
- Three.js uses Y-up, right-handed coordinate system.
- Blender uses Z-up, right-handed coordinate system.
- To convert: apply a -90° rotation around the X-axis to the entire scene.

# Three.js Scene Graph Traversal and Geometry Extraction

## Overview
Three.js organizes 3D objects in a scene graph (tree of `Object3D` nodes). To export geometry, you must traverse this tree, accumulate world transforms, and extract vertex/face data from each `Mesh`.

## Scene Graph Traversal
```javascript
// Ensure all world matrices are computed
root.updateMatrixWorld(true);

// Traverse all descendants
root.traverse((child) => {
  if (child.isMesh) {
    // child.matrixWorld contains the full world transform
    const geometry = child.geometry;
    // Process geometry...
  }
});
```

## Extracting Geometry Data
Three.js `BufferGeometry` stores data in typed arrays:

```javascript
const geometry = mesh.geometry;
const position = geometry.getAttribute('position'); // Float32BufferAttribute
const normal = geometry.getAttribute('normal');       // may be null
const index = geometry.index;                          // may be null (non-indexed)

// Vertices
for (let i = 0; i < position.count; i++) {
  const x = position.getX(i);
  const y = position.getY(i);
  const z = position.getZ(i);
}

// Faces (indexed geometry)
if (index) {
  for (let i = 0; i < index.count; i += 3) {
    const a = index.getX(i);
    const b = index.getX(i + 1);
    const c = index.getX(i + 2);
  }
}
```

## Applying World Transform to Vertices
To get vertices in world space, apply the mesh's `matrixWorld`:

```javascript
const vertex = new THREE.Vector3();
const normalVec = new THREE.Vector3();
const normalMatrix = new THREE.Matrix3().getNormalMatrix(mesh.matrixWorld);

for (let i = 0; i < position.count; i++) {
  vertex.set(position.getX(i), position.getY(i), position.getZ(i));
  vertex.applyMatrix4(mesh.matrixWorld);
  // vertex now in world coords

  if (normal) {
    normalVec.set(normal.getX(i), normal.getY(i), normal.getZ(i));
    normalVec.applyMatrix3(normalMatrix).normalize();
  }
}
```

## Handling InstancedMesh
`InstancedMesh` requires special handling — each instance has its own transform matrix:

```javascript
if (child.isInstancedMesh) {
  const instanceMatrix = new THREE.Matrix4();
  for (let i = 0; i < child.count; i++) {
    child.getMatrixAt(i, instanceMatrix);
    // Combine: worldMatrix = child.matrixWorld * instanceMatrix
    const worldMatrix = child.matrixWorld.clone().multiply(instanceMatrix);
    // Apply worldMatrix to each vertex of the geometry
  }
}
```

## Handling Non-Uniform & Negative Scales
When a mesh has negative scale (mirrored geometry), face winding must be reversed to maintain correct normals:

```javascript
const det = worldMatrix.determinant();
if (det < 0) {
  // Reverse face winding order: swap indices b and c
  // for each triangle (a, b, c) -> (a, c, b)
}
```

The normal matrix for non-uniform scales must be the transpose of the inverse of the upper-left 3x3:
```javascript
const normalMatrix = new THREE.Matrix3().getNormalMatrix(worldMatrix);
```

# OBJ File Format Reference

## Overview
The Wavefront OBJ format is a simple text-based 3D geometry format widely supported by 3D applications including Blender. It stores vertices, normals, texture coordinates, and face definitions.

## Basic Structure
```obj
# Comment line
o ObjectName

# Vertices (v x y z)
v 1.0 2.0 3.0
v 4.0 5.0 6.0

# Vertex normals (vn nx ny nz)
vn 0.0 1.0 0.0
vn 0.0 0.0 1.0

# Texture coordinates (vt u v)
vt 0.5 0.5

# Faces (f v1/vt1/vn1 v2/vt2/vn2 v3/vt3/vn3)
# or  (f v1//vn1 v2//vn2 v3//vn3)  -- no texture coords
# or  (f v1 v2 v3)                  -- vertex only
f 1//1 2//1 3//2
```

## Important Rules
1. **1-based indexing**: Vertex/normal/texcoord indices start at 1, not 0.
2. **Global indices**: Indices are global across the entire file, not per-object.
3. **Face winding**: Counter-clockwise winding for front faces (standard).
4. **Groups**: `g groupname` or `o objectname` to organize geometry.

## Writing OBJ from Code
```javascript
let objContent = '';
let vertexOffset = 1; // OBJ is 1-based

for (const mesh of meshes) {
  objContent += `o ${mesh.name}\n`;

  // Write vertices
  for (const v of mesh.vertices) {
    objContent += `v ${v.x} ${v.y} ${v.z}\n`;
  }

  // Write normals
  for (const n of mesh.normals) {
    objContent += `vn ${n.x} ${n.y} ${n.z}\n`;
  }

  // Write faces (with offset for global indexing)
  for (const face of mesh.faces) {
    const a = face[0] + vertexOffset;
    const b = face[1] + vertexOffset;
    const c = face[2] + vertexOffset;
    objContent += `f ${a}//${a} ${b}//${b} ${c}//${c}\n`;
  }

  vertexOffset += mesh.vertices.length;
}
```

## Coordinate System Conversion (Three.js Y-up to Blender Z-up)
Apply a -90° rotation around the X-axis:
```
x' = x
y' = z
z' = -y
```

Matrix form (rotation of -90° about X):
```
[1,  0,  0]   [x]   [ x]
[0,  0,  1] × [y] = [ z]
[0, -1,  0]   [z]   [-y]
```

This is equivalent to: `new_x = x, new_y = z, new_z = -y`

Apply this to both vertex positions and normals.

# Three.js OBJ Exporter (Custom Node.js Implementation)

## Overview
While Three.js provides an `OBJExporter` addon, for precise control over coordinate system conversion and handling edge cases (InstancedMesh, negative scales), a custom exporter is often more reliable in Node.js.

## Complete Export Pipeline

### Step 1: Build the scene
```javascript
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { createScene } from './object.js';
const root = createScene();
```

### Step 2: Apply coordinate system rotation
For Blender Z-up, apply -90° X-rotation to the root before computing world matrices:
```javascript
const conversionRoot = new THREE.Group();
conversionRoot.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
conversionRoot.add(root);
conversionRoot.updateMatrixWorld(true);
```

### Step 3: Traverse and collect geometry
```javascript
const meshDataList = [];

conversionRoot.traverse((child) => {
  if (child.isInstancedMesh) {
    // Handle each instance as a separate mesh entry
    for (let i = 0; i < child.count; i++) {
      const instanceMatrix = new THREE.Matrix4();
      child.getMatrixAt(i, instanceMatrix);
      const worldMatrix = child.matrixWorld.clone().multiply(instanceMatrix);
      meshDataList.push(extractGeometry(child.geometry, worldMatrix, `${child.name}_${i}`));
    }
  } else if (child.isMesh) {
    meshDataList.push(extractGeometry(child.geometry, child.matrixWorld, child.name));
  }
});
```

### Step 4: Extract geometry with world transforms
```javascript
function extractGeometry(geometry, worldMatrix, name) {
  const position = geometry.getAttribute('position');
  const normal = geometry.getAttribute('normal');
  const index = geometry.index;

  const normalMatrix = new THREE.Matrix3().getNormalMatrix(worldMatrix);
  const det = worldMatrix.determinant();

  const vertices = [];
  const normals = [];
  const faces = [];

  const v = new THREE.Vector3();
  const n = new THREE.Vector3();

  for (let i = 0; i < position.count; i++) {
    v.fromBufferAttribute(position, i).applyMatrix4(worldMatrix);
    vertices.push({ x: v.x, y: v.y, z: v.z });

    if (normal) {
      n.fromBufferAttribute(normal, i).applyMatrix3(normalMatrix).normalize();
      normals.push({ x: n.x, y: n.y, z: n.z });
    }
  }

  if (index) {
    for (let i = 0; i < index.count; i += 3) {
      let a = index.getX(i), b = index.getX(i+1), c = index.getX(i+2);
      if (det < 0) [b, c] = [c, b]; // fix winding for negative scale
      faces.push([a, b, c]);
    }
  } else {
    for (let i = 0; i < position.count; i += 3) {
      let a = i, b = i+1, c = i+2;
      if (det < 0) [b, c] = [c, b];
      faces.push([a, b, c]);
    }
  }

  return { name, vertices, normals, faces };
}
```

### Step 5: Write OBJ file
```javascript
import { writeFileSync } from 'fs';

let obj = '# Exported from Three.js\n';
let vOffset = 1;
let vnOffset = 1;

for (const data of meshDataList) {
  obj += `o ${data.name}\n`;
  for (const v of data.vertices) {
    obj += `v ${v.x.toFixed(6)} ${v.y.toFixed(6)} ${v.z.toFixed(6)}\n`;
  }
  for (const n of data.normals) {
    obj += `vn ${n.x.toFixed(6)} ${n.y.toFixed(6)} ${n.z.toFixed(6)}\n`;
  }
  for (const f of data.faces) {
    if (data.normals.length > 0) {
      obj += `f ${f[0]+vOffset}//${f[0]+vnOffset} ${f[1]+vOffset}//${f[1]+vnOffset} ${f[2]+vOffset}//${f[2]+vnOffset}\n`;
    } else {
      obj += `f ${f[0]+vOffset} ${f[1]+vOffset} ${f[2]+vOffset}\n`;
    }
  }
  vOffset += data.vertices.length;
  vnOffset += data.normals.length;
}

writeFileSync('output/object.obj', obj);
```

## Edge Cases
